# Target C-II — singleton/full-rhombic-pentagon-flower dichotomy extends through `t<=2.02`

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only scope propagation / high-`t` structural strengthening  
**Scope:** canonical full-core Type-II train-measure branch  
**Dependencies:** hostile-audited uniform all-train gap `g=0.007707`; exact singleton/full-flower incidence inequality; modern train-slack identity; isolate-host face degree-`>=7` theorem  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_full_pentagon_flower_extended_range.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The modern singleton/full-pentagon-flower theorem was originally tabulated only through

\[
t={q-k\over D}\le1.8623.
\]

That endpoint came from the then-current pentagon/path discussion.  Its two actual inputs are broader:

1. the all-train pentagon floor is available throughout the full-core Type-II train-measure branch; and
2. the exact finite inequality
   \[
   P_5-F_5^{full}\le2S
   \]
   has no `t` restriction.

Therefore the same dichotomy persists throughout the full range where the uniform pentagon lower envelope is positive, namely every fixed

\[
\boxed{t<2.02157\ldots .}
\]

At the useful round endpoint `t<=2.000`, every survivor has

\[
\boxed{
S>0.0179D-o(D)
\quad\text{or}\quad
F_5^{full}>0.0179D-o(D).
}
\tag{FF.1}
\]

All fully rhombic pentagons in the second branch are source-empty.  If that branch occurs, then additionally

\[
\boxed{\sigma_R>1.457D-o(D),}
\tag{FF.2}
\]

\[
\boxed{{T_R^2\over4}-R_4>0.826D^2-o(D^2),}
\tag{FF.3}
\]

and more than

\[
\boxed{0.0449D-o(D)}
\tag{FF.4}
\]

distinct strict-rhombus stopped trains terminate on fully rhombic pentagonal flowers.

Even at `t<=2.020` the common singleton/full-flower floor remains positive.

---

## 1. Uniform pentagon floor

The scope-safe matched all-train theorem gives throughout the full-core train-measure branch

\[
\liminf{T_{train}-q/2\over D}
\ge C_{707}:=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.007707,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\tag{FF.5}
\]

Canonical stopped-train and loaded-hull bookkeeping then gives, at any fixed endpoint `t<=X`,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {P_5\over D}
\ge p_5(X):=C_{707}+\pi-{5X\over2}.
}
\tag{FF.6}
\]

The right side is positive for

\[
X<{2(C_{707}+\pi)\over5}=2.02157\ldots .
\tag{FF.7}
\]

The exact companion certificate gives a lower enclosure of the threshold above `2.0215`.

---

## 2. Exact finite full-flower incidence inequality

Let `S` be the number of singleton stopped trains, equivalently contact edges incident to no strict-rhombus face.  Let `F_5^{full}` count bounded pentagons with a strict unit rhombus across each of all five sides.

If a pentagon is not fully rhombic, at least one of its boundary edges has no strict rhombus on either side, because the pentagon itself is not a rhombus.  That edge is singleton.  One singleton edge has only two face sides, so it can witness non-fullness of at most two pentagons.

Hence finitely

\[
\boxed{P_5-F_5^{full}\le2S.}
\tag{FF.8}
\]

This is the same exact inequality as in the parent theorem and has no asymptotic or phase hypothesis.

---

## 3. Balanced dichotomy at an arbitrary fixed endpoint

Define

\[
s(X):={p_5(X)\over3}.
\tag{FF.9}
\]

If

\[
S\ge s(X)D-o(D),
\]

the singleton alternative holds.  Otherwise, using (FF.6) and (FF.8),

\[
\begin{aligned}
F_5^{full}
&\ge P_5-2S\\
&>p_5(X)D-2s(X)D-o(D)\\
&=s(X)D-o(D).
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
\max\{S,F_5^{full}\}
\ge s(X)D-o(D).
}
\tag{FF.10}
\]

Safe exact rows are

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
X & s(X)\text{ lower}\\ \hline
1.880 & 0.1179\\
1.900 & 0.1013\\
1.920 & 0.0846\\
1.950 & 0.0596\\
1.960 & 0.0513\\
2.000 & 0.0179\\
2.020 & 0.0013.
\end{array}
\tag{FF.11}

Every fully rhombic pentagon in (FF.10) has empty source interior by the merged isolate-host theorem.

---

## 4. Failure of the singleton alternative forces very large rhombic train slack

The exact stopped-train split gives

\[
\rho_{rec}=2S,
\qquad
2\tau_D=\rho_{rec}+\sigma_R,
\tag{FF.12}
\]

and the hostile-audited modern theorem gives

\[
\tau_D\ge(\tau_0-o(1))D,
\qquad
\tau_0=0.7467921.
\tag{FF.13}
\]

In the full-flower side of (FF.10),

\[
S<s(X)D+o(D),
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\sigma_R\over D}
\ge \sigma_0(X):=2[\tau_0-s(X)].
}
\tag{FF.14}
\]

Representative safe lower rows are

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
X & \sigma_0(X)\\ \hline
1.880 & 1.257\\
1.900 & 1.290\\
1.920 & 1.324\\
1.950 & 1.374\\
1.960 & 1.390\\
2.000 & 1.457\\
2.020 & 1.490.
\end{array}
\tag{FF.15}

The increasing lower bound is not paradoxical: as the forced pentagon population shrinks, the balanced alternative permits a smaller singleton count; failure of that smaller singleton alternative forces more of the fixed total train slack into `sigma_R`.

---

## 5. Quadratic Mantel deficit in the full-flower branch

For the strict-rhombus stopped-train crossing graph,

\[
\sigma_R=2T_R-4\sqrt{R_4}
\]

and exactly

\[
\boxed{
{T_R^2\over4}-R_4
={\sigma_R\sqrt{R_4}\over2}
+{\sigma_R^2\over16}.
}
\tag{FF.16}
\]

Since

\[
R_4/D^2\to\Delta_2,
\]

(FF.14) gives

\[
\liminf D^{-2}\left({T_R^2\over4}-R_4\right)
\ge {\sigma_0(X)\sqrt{\Delta_2}\over2}
+{\sigma_0(X)^2\over16}.
\tag{FF.17}
\]

The exact certificate gives safe rows

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
X & \text{Mantel deficit}/D^2\text{ lower}\\ \hline
1.880 & 0.697\\
1.900 & 0.718\\
1.920 & 0.740\\
1.950 & 0.772\\
1.960 & 0.783\\
2.000 & 0.826\\
2.020 & 0.848.
\end{array}
\tag{FF.18}

---

## 6. Fully rhombic flowers force terminal train mass

A fully rhombic pentagon has five terminal strict-rhombus face-side occurrences.  A complete stopped train has at most two terminal occurrences.  Therefore, if `N_full` is the number of distinct non-singleton stopped trains ending at fully rhombic pentagons,

\[
\boxed{N_{full}\ge{5\over2}F_5^{full}.}
\tag{FF.19}
\]

In the full-flower alternative this gives

\[
\liminf {N_{full}\over D}
\ge {5\over2}s(X).
\tag{FF.20}
\]

Safe rows are

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
X & N_{full}/D\text{ lower}\\ \hline
1.880 & 0.2949\\
1.900 & 0.2532\\
1.920 & 0.2116\\
1.950 & 0.1491\\
1.960 & 0.1282\\
2.000 & 0.0449\\
2.020 & 0.0032.
\end{array}
\tag{FF.21}

---

## 7. Frontier effect

The modern full-core high-`t` train network therefore has the following scope-independent hierarchy:

```text
for every fixed t < 2.02157...:
    either a linear singleton-train population
    or a linear population of source-empty fully rhombic pentagonal flowers;

in the full-flower branch:
    a correspondingly large rhombic train-isoperimetric slack;
    a quadratic Mantel deficit;
    linearly many rhombic trains terminating on the flowers.
```

This remains separate from the corrected O--O path/hub theorem, whose current endpoint is `t<=1.860`.  No hub or finite-strip conclusion is imported above that range.

Local metric regressions still prevent assigning a uniform angular defect to one fully rhombic flower.  The closing target is therefore network-theoretic: exploit the incidence graph between many empty five-rhombus holes and the highly non-Mantel rhombic train system, or exploit the singleton endpoint graph in the opposite branch.

**Disposition:** proved scope propagation of the singleton/full-flower dichotomy through `t<=2.02`, with exact train-slack and Mantel consequences. C-II and Level I remain open.
